#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""eyes — point your phone camera at the world, frames land on disk.

A local-first way to give a terminal AI agent (or anything that can read a
file) live eyes: your iPhone opens a web page served from this machine, and
every captured frame is written to a stable path
(~/.local/state/eyes/latest.jpg) with an atomic replace, plus a rolling
timestamped history. No app, no App Store, no cloud storage.

Zero dependencies: Python stdlib + the system `openssl` binary (cert
generation) + libcrypto via ctypes (AES-GCM). The phone side is one
self-contained HTML page (inlined below, no CDN assets).

Two modes:

  LAN (default)   — HTTPS on your LAN address with a locally generated CA.
                    Nothing ever leaves your network.

  remote (opt-in) — a Cloudflare Tunnel (outbound-only, no port forwarding)
                    exposes the page at your own hostname. Frames are
                    END-TO-END ENCRYPTED in the browser (WebCrypto
                    AES-256-GCM) with a key carried in the URL *fragment* —
                    browsers never send the fragment to the server, so the
                    key never reaches Cloudflare and the tunnel only ever
                    relays ciphertext. Decryption happens here, on disk
                    frames are plain JPEGs.

Usage:
    eyes serve [--port 8443] [--bind IP] [--remote] [--fresh-token]
    eyes latest                     # print path of the newest frame
    eyes wait [--timeout SECS]      # block until a NEW frame arrives
    eyes gc [--keep N] [--max-mb M] # prune history, printing what goes
    eyes tunnel setup HOSTNAME      # one-time Cloudflare Tunnel setup
    eyes tunnel up|down|status      # start/stop the tunnel (user systemd)

Why HTTPS + a private CA on the LAN: iOS Safari refuses to expose
navigator.mediaDevices (camera) unless the page is a *fully trusted* secure
context — merely tapping through a self-signed-cert warning is NOT enough on
iOS. So `eyes` generates a local CA once, issues a leaf cert for the LAN IP,
and the phone page walks you through trusting the CA (a one-time ~60s dance
in iOS Settings). When the LAN IP changes, only the leaf is re-issued — no
re-trusting. In remote mode Cloudflare's edge presents a real publicly
trusted certificate, so none of this is needed on the phone.

Security model (full version in the README): LAN-address bind only, an
unguessable token on every path, size-capped uploads that must parse as
JPEG/encrypted-frame, a global rate limit, capped history. Remote mode adds
Cloudflare Access (login wall) + the app token + the E2E key, and refuses
plaintext frames entirely.
"""

